2. PRODUCT DESIGN PRINCIPLES

These principles must guide the entire development process.

2.1 Minimalist Interface

The application should look like a professionally designed commercial product.

Avoid unnecessary text, descriptions, decorative headings, explanations and visual clutter.

For example:

- Do not write a paragraph explaining what the Dashboard does.
- Do not add descriptions under every page title.
- Do not explain obvious buttons with tooltips.
- Do not display unnecessary empty-state instructions.
- Do not repeat information already visible elsewhere.
- Do not use large decorative cards when a compact layout works better.

Use short, meaningful labels.

Instead of:

"Here is an overview of all your business activities and important information."

Use:

"Overview"

Instead of:

"Click this button to create a new client."

Use:

"New Client"

The interface should assume that the user can understand ordinary application conventions.

2.2 Avoid AI-Generated Interface Patterns

Do not make the application look like an AI-generated dashboard.

Avoid:

- Excessive rounded cards.
- Unnecessary gradients.
- Random decorative icons.
- Oversized headings.
- Excessive empty space.
- Repetitive descriptions.
- Artificial-looking statistics.
- Unnecessary AI assistant panels.
- Emojis used as interface icons.
- Decorative charts that communicate no useful information.

Use a restrained, consistent visual system.

The interface should feel intentional, mature and practical.

2.3 Information Density

The desktop application should make efficient use of available screen space.

Where appropriate, use:

- Tables.
- Compact lists.
- Split views.
- Side panels.
- Tabs.
- Expandable sections.
- Contextual action menus.
- Search and filtering.

Avoid forcing users to open multiple pages to compare information that could reasonably appear together.

2.4 Responsive Design

Do not simply shrink the desktop interface to fit a phone.

Each platform should have a layout designed around its actual use.

